// ---- correlated aggregate subqueries, every key at once ----------------------
//
// `(SELECT SUM(ls.quantity) FROM locstock ls WHERE ls.stockid = sm.stockid)`
// in a select list or WHERE over 30k items ran the subquery once per item.
// It is one grouped query instead - `SELECT ls.stockid, SUM(ls.quantity) FROM
// locstock ls WHERE <its other conditions> GROUP BY ls.stockid` - run once,
// and each outer row reads its key's value (no key: what the aggregate makes
// of no rows, 0 for COUNT, NULL for the rest). The groups are the subquery's
// row sets for every key value (grouped by the same equality the WHERE uses),
// added up in the same row order. Kept until the table changes. Taken after
// the first few outer rows only: a statement that asks for one row keeps its
// one indexed lookup.
